So do I, but I use NeXT's compiler, and I'm not sure it handles this the
way it is described in Cox & Novobilski.

As I understand C&N, that notation does more than static typing -- it does
static BINDING:

	This changes the code that the compiler generates for all
	messages involving those variables.  Calls through the
	messager that would resolve the message binding at runtime
	are replaced by indirect function calls to the actual method.

The way I read this, if you have B a subclass of A, then:

	A* x;
	B* y;

	y = [B new];
	x = y;

	[x someMethod];

will always call A's -someMethod, even if B redefines it.

I had always wanted Objective C to have static typing, but I wanted it to
constrain dynamic binding to subclasses, not to eliminate it altogether.

NeXT's compiler does what I want:

